 "text": "\n\n<｜DSML｜function_calls\n\n<｜DSML｜function_calls\n\n<｜DSML｜function_callsBased on my medical knowledge:\n\nType II supracondylar humerus fractures are classified as Gartland Type II fractures, which are displaced fractures with an intact posterior cortex. The current standard of care for Type II supracondylar humerus fractures in children is typically operative treatment (closed reduction and percutaneous pinning) because they are unstable and have a high risk of displacement with nonoperative treatment. However, some studies suggest that certain Type II fractures with minimal displacement and good alignment might be managed nonoperatively with close monitoring.\n\nGiven the standard practice and risk of complications like cubitus varus with nonoperative management, most Type II fractures require surgical intervention.\n\n{\"answer\": \"maybe\", \"confidence\": 75}",
